        <p>
          Never quote a single flat price for one deliverable. Offer three structured options in your pitch (e.g., <em>Option A: 1 Dedicated Reel ($500)</em> | <em>Option B: 1 Reel + 3 Stories + Bio Link ($700)</em> | <em>Option C: 2 Reels + 6 Stories + 30-Day Paid Whitelisting ($1,200)</em>). Packaging anchors your value and encourages brands to choose higher-tier options.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="step-item">
        <h3>2. Itemize and Charge for Usage Rights & Licensing</h3>
        <p>
          Your baseline quote covers organic posting on your profile only. If a brand wants to run your video as a paid ad (Meta Partnership Ads or TikTok Spark Ads), charge an additional <strong>30% to 50% licensing fee per 30-day usage window</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="step-item">
        <h3>3. Protect Your Schedule with Rush Fees & Revision Limits</h3>
        <p>
          Include clear boundaries in your contract: specify that the fee includes <strong>one round of minor creative revisions</strong>, and charge a <strong>25% to 50% rush fee</strong> for campaigns requiring content turnaround in under 7 business days.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="step-item">
        <h3>4. Negotiate Deliverables Instead of Dropping Price</h3>
        <p>
          If a brand says your rate is over their budget, do not simply discount your price. Instead, reduce the deliverables to match their budget (e.g., <em>'If your budget is $400 instead of $600, I can provide 1 Reel and 1 Story instead of the full 3-Story package.'</em>). This preserves your rate integrity.
        </p>
